MobileLLM-R1 sits between AutoScale (rejected at 5.5) and OpenMathInstruct-2 (accepted at 6.5). The paper is clearly stronger than AutoScale: the empirical results are more convincing, the pipeline is more complete, and the open-source value is higher. However, the misleading headline claim and the absence of baselines for the core methodological contribution are real issues that are analogous to the reasons AutoScale was rejected. Against OpenMathInstruct-2, the paper is comparable in open-source value and ablation breadth, but OpenMathInstruct-2 does not have the same structural issue with its central claim.

The paper lands at **6.0** — borderline accept. The genuine contributions (LOO analysis, staged post-training insights, open-source recipe, competitive performance against larger models) clear the bar for acceptance, but the headline claim requires correction and the influence-based curation needs at minimum one stronger baseline.

